(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-venous-disorders-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Venous Disorders Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Within what interval should superficial thrombophlebitis be reexamined?","choices":["3 months","7 to 10 days","24 hours","3 to 4 weeks"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Superficial thrombophlebitis is reexamined within 7 to 10 days to assess resolution versus progression. If signs and symptoms persist or worsen at that visit, a repeat duplex ultrasound is ordered. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"What is the most common predisposing factor for varicose veins?","choices":["Pregnancy","Family history","Prolonged standing","Heavy lifting"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Family history is the most common predisposing factor for developing unwanted leg veins. Periods of high venous pressure from prolonged standing or heavy lifting, and pregnancy in women, are also risk factors, but none is as common. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which finding makes outpatient therapy for deep vein thrombosis inappropriate?","choices":["Reliable follow-up","Normal kidney function","Iliofemoral (proximal) clot","Stable vital signs"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An iliofemoral (proximal) deep vein thrombosis (DVT) is one of the findings that makes outpatient therapy inappropriate, along with concurrent symptomatic pulmonary embolism, high bleeding risk and comorbidities that warrant inpatient care. Every other choice here is on the list that supports outpatient treatment. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Where does the itching of chronic venous disease often begin?","choices":["Around the lateral malleolus","Over the dorsum of the foot","Behind the knee in the popliteal fossa","Around the medial malleolus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pruritus of the skin of the lower extremities in chronic venous disease often starts around the medial malleolus, the inner ankle. It can come with leg heaviness, aching or swelling that worsens with standing and is relieved by elevation and walking. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"How reliable is the Homan sign (calf pain when the foot is flexed upward) for deep vein thrombosis?","choices":["Both sensitive and specific","Neither sensitive nor specific","Sensitive but not specific","Specific but not sensitive"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A patient with deep vein thrombosis (DVT) may have a positive Homan sign, but it is neither sensitive nor specific. Swelling (97% sensitive), pain (86%) and warmth (72%) are the more useful presenting findings. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Septic thrombophlebitis is uncommon without which history?","choices":["Recent long-haul air travel","Estrogen-containing contraception","Advanced chronic kidney disease","Venous cannulation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Septic thrombophlebitis is uncommon in the absence of a history of venous cannulation, such as venipuncture or catheterization. Recent intravenous catheter use is also the most common cause of upper extremity superficial thrombophlebitis. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"How many points does an alternative diagnosis at least as likely as deep vein thrombosis subtract in the Wells criteria?","choices":["One point","Two points","Three points","Five points"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An alternative diagnosis at least as likely as deep vein thrombosis (DVT) scores minus 2, the only negative item in the Wells criteria. Every other item adds 1 point, and the total sorts patients into low (zero or less), moderate (1 to 2) or high (3 or more) likelihood. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which age group carries a higher risk of deep vein thrombosis?","choices":["Older than 60 years","Younger than 20 years","Younger than 30 years","Aged 20 to 40 years"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Age over 60 years is listed among the risk factors for deep vein thrombosis (DVT), along with being overweight or obese, heart failure, cancer, chronic kidney disease and a sedentary lifestyle. A personal or family history of DVT or pulmonary embolism also raises risk. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"After parenteral therapy for 5 days, which agents can anticoagulation be switched to?","choices":["Rivaroxaban or apixaban","Warfarin or heparin","Aspirin or clopidogrel","Dabigatran or edoxaban"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In the second of the three anticoagulation strategies, parenteral therapy is switched after 5 days to a novel anticoagulant: dabigatran, a direct thrombin inhibitor, or edoxaban, an anti-Xa agent. Rivaroxaban and apixaban differ because they are given orally from the start with a loading dose. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Pulmonary embolism complicates up to what share of upper extremity deep vein thromboses?","choices":["15% to 30%","40% to 50%","Almost never","Up to 6%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonary embolism occurs in up to 6% of upper extremity deep vein thromboses (DVTs), compared with 15% to 30% of lower extremity DVTs. Upper extremity clots are only 5% to 10% of all DVTs and are most commonly due to pacemakers, implanted defibrillators or central venous catheters. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What is the primary presenting symptom of chronic venous insufficiency?","choices":["Cool, pale toes","Numbness across the foot","Progressive pitting edema","Calf pain when walking"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Progressive pitting edema of the leg, usually the lower leg, is the primary presenting symptom of chronic venous insufficiency. It usually comes with dull discomfort worsened by standing, and pruritic dermatitis and brownish pigmentation follow over time. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which history typically accompanies lymphedema when it mimics deep vein thrombosis?","choices":["A recent long flight","Starting a new blood pressure drug","A sudden twisting injury","Pelvic surgery or radiation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Lymphedema is chronic edema of the extremities that is usually accompanied by a history of pelvic surgery, malignancy or radiation therapy. That history is the clue that separates it from deep vein thrombosis (DVT) on the differential of a swollen leg. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Without treatment, what is the cumulative rate of fatal pulmonary embolism in symptomatic deep vein thrombosis without pulmonary embolism?","choices":["Under 1%","About 3%","About 9%","About 25%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Without adequate treatment, the cumulative rate of fatal pulmonary embolism is estimated at under 1% in symptomatic deep vein thrombosis (DVT) without pulmonary embolism. It rises to 3% with symptomatic nonmassive pulmonary embolism and about 9% with symptomatic massive pulmonary embolism. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"What defines superficial phlebitis?","choices":["Vein wall inflammation without a clot","Vein wall inflammation with a clot","Infected vein wall with pus","Clot within a deep leg vein"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Superficial phlebitis is inflammation of the wall of a vein in the absence of thrombus. When a clot is present in the inflamed vein, the condition is called superficial thrombophlebitis, so the presence or absence of clot is the dividing line. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"In the Wells criteria, calf swelling counts when it exceeds what difference from the other leg?","choices":["More than 1 cm","More than 3 cm","More than 5 cm","More than 10 cm"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Calf swelling scores 1 point when the calf is more than 3 cm larger than the other leg, measured 10 cm below the tibial tuberosity. Measuring at that fixed point keeps the comparison between the two legs consistent. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Under the Virchow triad, cancer belongs to which component?","choices":["Venous stasis","Endothelial trauma","None of the three","Hypercoagulable state"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cancer is listed under the hypercoagulable state of the Virchow triad, an acquired clotting tendency alongside oral contraceptives, postmenopausal estrogen replacement and pregnancy. Inherited examples in the same component include factor V Leiden and protein C or S deficiency. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which component of the Virchow triad includes postoperative inactivity?","choices":["Venous stasis","Hypercoagulable state","Endothelial trauma","Turbulent arterial flow"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Immobilization, including long flights, prolonged sitting and postoperative inactivity, is listed under venous stasis, the altered blood flow component of the Virchow triad. Immobilization carries about a 20-fold increased risk for deep vein thrombosis (DVT). — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"For which clot may thrombolysis be considered in deep vein thrombosis?","choices":["A large proximal clot","An isolated calf clot","A superficial vein clot","Any clot with active bleeding"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Thrombolysis with tissue plasminogen activator (tPA) is helpful for a large proximal deep vein thrombosis (DVT), or for a pulmonary embolism in a hemodynamically stable patient. Thrombectomy is the other option for removing clot directly. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"For how long after delivery does postpartum status raise deep vein thrombosis risk?","choices":["The first 6 to 12 weeks","The first 2 weeks only","The first 6 months","The first 2 years"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The first 6 to 12 weeks postpartum is listed among the risk factors for deep vein thrombosis (DVT). Pregnancy itself is a hypercoagulable state in the Virchow triad, and the raised risk carries through those weeks after delivery. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Advancing age raises deep vein thrombosis risk above which age?","choices":["30 years","50 years","60 years","75 years"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Age over 60 years is listed among the other risk factors for deep vein thrombosis (DVT), alongside being overweight or obese, smoking, heart failure and a personal or family history of DVT or pulmonary embolism. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What is the purpose of emollients in venous skin care?","choices":["Shrink the dilated veins","Dissolve deeper skin scarring","Prevent new clot formation","Maintain the skin barrier"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Emollients such as Vaseline or Lubriderm maintain the skin barrier, preventing dryness and fissuring and reducing scratching. Skin care is part of initial nonoperative management of chronic venous insufficiency, along with leg elevation, exercise and compression. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"How is an arteriovenous fistula of the extremity diagnosed?","choices":["By skin punch biopsy","By ankle-brachial index","By physical examination","By D-dimer measurement"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The diagnosis of an arteriovenous (AV) fistula is determined by physical examination. Computed tomography angiography and angiography can then confirm it and give the location and size, and duplex ultrasound confirms suspected iatrogenic fistulas after groin access. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"Which inherited hypercoagulable state is listed among the Virchow triad risk factors?","choices":["Estrogen replacement","Factor V Leiden","Cancer","Pregnancy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Factor V Leiden is an inherited hypercoagulable state, listed with prothrombin gene mutation and protein C, protein S and antithrombin deficiencies. The other choices are hypercoagulable too, but acquired rather than inherited. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Where should a patient with a venous ulcer be referred for care?","choices":["A pain management clinic","Wound care management","A hematology clinic","A rheumatology clinic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Venous ulcers are referred to wound care management, where complete healing can take 4 to 6 months. Ulcers will not heal unless edema is controlled and compression is applied, and an ulcer that does not fully heal needs further workup for infection or other causes. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"An elevated D-dimer implies increased activity of which process?","choices":["Vitamin K depletion","Red cell destruction","Fibrinolysis","Platelet production"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. D-dimer elevation implies increased fibrinolysis, the breakdown of fibrin clot. Because it is sensitive but not specific, it serves as a rule-out test for deep vein thrombosis (DVT) and pulmonary embolism: a negative result in a low-risk patient ends the work-up, while a positive one leads to ultrasound. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"For superficial thrombophlebitis, which situation prompts adding anticoagulation?","choices":["Pain relieved by warm compresses","Extensive clot or higher thrombosis risk","Any tender palpable cord in a leg","Purulent drainage over the vein"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Anticoagulation may be started when the clot burden is extensive or the patient is at intermediate or higher risk of thrombosis, using unfractionated heparin, enoxaparin or fondaparinux. Otherwise warm compresses, compression, elevation and anti-inflammatory drugs are the typical treatment. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"How is the D-dimer characterized as a test for deep vein thrombosis?","choices":["Both sensitive and specific","Neither sensitive nor specific","Sensitive but not specific","Specific but not sensitive"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. D-dimer is sensitive but not specific: elevation implies increased fibrinolysis, so a positive result can occur without a clot. That profile makes it a rule-out test for deep vein thrombosis (DVT) and pulmonary embolism, where a negative result in a low-risk patient ends the work-up. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"In the low likelihood Wells category, what happens after a negative D-dimer?","choices":["The test is repeated in a week","The work-up stops","Duplex ultrasound follows","Venography is performed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In the low-likelihood Wells category (score zero or less), a negative D-dimer ends the work-up. The test is sensitive, so a negative result reliably rules out deep vein thrombosis (DVT) in these patients, while a positive one would lead to ultrasound. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"After a negative thigh ultrasound in a high likelihood patient, which follow-up step is appropriate?","choices":["Repeat thigh ultrasound in a week","Discharge with no follow-up","Immediate thrombolysis","Inferior vena cava filter placement"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In a high-likelihood patient, a negative thigh ultrasound does not end the work-up. The next options are an ultrasound of the lower leg, phlebography, or a repeat ultrasound of the thigh in 1 week. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What share of all deep vein thromboses occur in the upper extremity?","choices":["5% to 10%","25% to 30%","40% to 50%","Under 1%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Upper extremity clots make up only 5% to 10% of all deep vein thromboses (DVTs), though their incidence is rising annually. Lower extremity clots are about 10 times more common, and arm clots are most often due to pacemakers, implanted defibrillators or central venous catheters. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 47"}]};
